# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# TEMPORARY — documentation set held out of version control on purpose.
#
# Reason: SPEC.md / AGENT-BRIEF.md / traceability/ / docs/ are intentionally NOT
# committed yet. They will be committed once the system is built.
#
# To commit the documentation set at that time:
#   1. delete the "Documentation set" block below (and nothing else)
#   2. git add -A && git commit
#
# Nothing is lost by ignoring: the files stay on disk and remain fully readable.
# NOTE: SPEC.md is the single source of truth and must NOT drift. While it is
# untracked there is no history to diff it against — keep your own backup.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# ── Documentation set ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
/SPEC.md
/AGENT-BRIEF.md
/README.md
/SESSION-HANDOFF.md
/traceability/
/docs/

# ── Operating-system / editor noise ─────────────────────────────────────────
Thumbs.db
ehthumbs.db
Desktop.ini
$RECYCLE.BIN/
*.lnk
.vscode/
.idea/
*.swp
*~

__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.venv/
# The real configuration, which holds the deployment's secrets. `apps/api/core/config.py`
# reads it automatically, so a checkout that ever placed one here would be a checkout with
# keys in it. `.env.example` is the tracked, secret-free template; this is the file a
# deployment fills in and never commits.
.env
# The throwaway environment a dependency audit builds to prove a clean install works.
# It exists only to be deleted, and must never reach a commit.
.verify-venv/
# Written into the working tree by any editable install (`pip install -e .`), whether
# declared or not. Build metadata, not source.
*.egg-info/
.pytest_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.ruff_cache/
htmlcov/
.coverage
# Written by any local run using --cov-report=xml. The coverage-gate job produces one on
# every CI run and uploads it as the coverage-xml artifact; a local one is never that
# artifact and must not be committed as if it were.
coverage.xml
# The persistent beat scheduler's schedule file, when the worker runs from this directory with
# the default WORKER_BEAT_SCHEDULE_PATH. It is state a deployment owns, not source; the CI job
# and any container write it to a path of their own.
/beat-schedule
# The gap scan's report, written by default wherever the scan is run from. It is output, not
# source: a report is regenerated on demand and carries the commit it was taken against, so a
# committed one would be stale the moment anything moved.
/gapscan-report.json

# ── Internal records — not part of the shipped codebase ─────────────────────
# The session handoff and the baseline-folder artifacts belong on disk, not in
# git history. SESSION-HANDOFF.md was previously (mistakenly) tracked.
/SESSION-HANDOFF.md
/E-G-forced-mismatch.txt
/SHA256SUMS

